1. Install and configure Windows Server Update Services (WSUS) on a Windows Server VM, ensuring the WSUS role is added and the initial setup wizard is completed.
=>1. Install and configure WSUS (role + initial setup wizard)

Open Server Manager → Manage → Add Roles and Features.
Choose Role-based or feature-based installation → select the local server.
On Server Roles, check Windows Server Update Services.
When prompted, add the required features
On the Content location page, specify a folder on a drive with plenty of free space .
Complete the installation and click Launch Post-Installation tasks .
Initial configuration wizard (starts automatically or via WSUS console):
Open Windows Server Update Services console .
Complete the wizard:
Choose Synchronize from Microsoft Updat.
Select languages (at least English + any others you need).
Choose products (Windows versions, Office, etc. that match your clients).
Choose classifications (at minimum: Critical Updates, Security Updates, Update Rollups, Service Packs).
Set the sync schedule (manual for the first sync, then scheduled).
Start the first synchronization

2.Create a group of at least two Windows client machines (physical or virtual), and configure their Windows Update settings via Group Policy to point to your WSUS server for updates.
=>On the WSUS server (optional but recommended):

In the WSUS console, expand Computers → All Computers.
Right-click → Add Computer Group → create a group (e.g. Training-Clients).

Configure clients with Group Policy (domain environment):

On a domain controller (or a machine with Group Policy Management), open Group Policy Management.
Create a new GPO (e.g. WSUS-Client-Config) and link it to the OU that contains your client computer accounts.
Edit the GPO and navigate to:
Computer Configuration → Policies → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Windows Update
Configure these key settings:
Specify intranet Microsoft update service location → Enabled
Set both “Set the intranet update service for detecting updates” and “Set the intranet statistics server” to:
http://Your-WSUS-Server:8530
(use https://Your-WSUS-Server:8531 if you configured SSL)

Configure Automatic Updates → Enabled (choose the install behavior you want, e.g. auto-download and schedule install).
Enable client-side targeting → Enabled and enter the group name you created in WSUS (e.g. Training-Clients) if you want automatic group assignment.

3. On your WSUS server, approve a specific Windows security update for installation on your client machines and initiate the update process.
=>In the WSUS console, go to Updates → All Updates.
Use the search or filters (Approval = Unapproved, Status = Needed, Classification = Security Updates) to find a specific security update that applies to your clients.
Right-click the update → Approve….
Select the computer group(s) you created (or All Computers) → set the approval to Approved for Install.
Click OK. Optionally set a deadline.
On the client machines, force a detection/install cycle:
Windows 10/11 / Server 2016+:
usoclient StartScan
or
usoclient StartInstall
Older clients: wuauclt /detectnow and wuauclt /updatenow
Monitor progress in the WSUS console under the update’s status or under each computer’s update status.

4.Generate a WSUS patch report that lists all client machines and shows which ones successfully installed the approved update, then export the report as a CSV or screenshot and save it for review.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the WSUS console's 'Reports' section to create and export the update status report.</em>
=>In the WSUS console, click Reports in the left pane.
Choose one of these useful reports:
Update Status Summary
Computer Status Summary
Update Detailed Status (best for seeing which machines installed a specific update)
Computer Detailed Status
Select the specific update you approved (or filter by group / time range).
Run the report.
Once generated, use the report viewer’s toolbar to:
Export as CSV, or
Print / save as PDF, or
Take a screenshot of the results.
Save the exported file for review.
